// SplitLiveRanges — the cold-spill live-range splitter (design: ARCHITECTURE.md's
// register-allocator section, Rule 2). It is what relieves COLD pressure, so the colorer
// never faces "pressure > pool": a value that is idle across a
// region of high register pressure is SPLIT — it lives in memory across the gap and in a
// register only where it is used — so the colorer then always finds `maxPressure ≤ pool`.
// HOT pressure (a value a loop USES that still cannot fit) is the E5001 diagnostic —
// `chooseVictim` returns `none` and the driver throws `raiseRegisterPressure` (M5.7,
// RegisterPressureDiagnostic.maxon).
//
// THE TWO INVARIANTS THIS RESTS ON (design Rules 1 + 2):
//
//  • BELADY SPLIT AT THE EVICTION POINT — a spilled value's live range is split ACROSS THE
//    PEAK, not shipped whole to memory. The store goes after the value's LAST use BEFORE the
//    peak (or at its def when it is idle before the peak); the reload goes before its NEXT
//    use AFTER the peak. Only the uses AT/AFTER the split point are rewritten to the fresh
//    reload id — the uses BEFORE it keep the ORIGINAL value in its original register. This is
//    what makes the value DEAD across the peak (freeing its register there), so a split
//    actually LOWERS peak pressure and the loop converges. Splitting whole-range and reloading
//    before a block's FIRST use (the earlier design) is WRONG: a value used both before AND
//    after a peak would get its fresh reload before the pre-peak use, leaving that fresh value
//    live ACROSS the peak — pressure never drops and `chooseVictim` re-picks the same lineage
//    forever.
//
//  • DOMINATING RELOADS — the key insight that retires v1's SplitKit failure with NO SSA
//    reconstruction. Every reload is placed in a use's own block, before the first AFTER-peak
//    use in that block — which DOMINATES the after-peak uses it serves. So no merge ever sees
//    two definitions, NO phi is ever needed, and each reload just DEFINES A FRESH ValueId whose
//    after-peak uses are rewritten to it (keeping the program in SSA and Rule 1 intact). The
//    price is occasional duplicate reloads (one per after-peak use-block), all provably COLD.
//
//  • RULE 2 (spill placement) — a store or reload may be inserted at a point `q` only if,
//    for every loop containing `q`, the value has NO use or def inside that loop. This is
//    what makes cold spills cold: spill code is NEVER added to a loop body for a value the
//    loop uses. We guarantee it by only ever choosing a victim whose def AND every use are
//    at loop depth 0 (`isColdSpillable`), and placing the store and each reload at (depth-0)
//    use/def sites — so no store/reload ever lands in any loop at all. `assertPlacementCold`
//    re-verifies this on every inserted op: a violation would be a HOT spill that should have
//    been E5001, so it panics rather than ship. A value idle across a loop it does not touch
//    is exactly the depth-0-def, depth-0-uses shape, split with ZERO instructions in the loop
//    body — the design's signature case.
//
// REMAT (load-bearing, not an optimization): an integer constant (`movRegImm`/`movRegImm32`)
// is REMATERIALIZED rather than spilled — re-emitted at each use with a fresh ValueId, never
// a register across the gap and never memory. A constant is always PREFERRED over spilling a
// real value (remat is free — no store, no slot, no reload). shv2 has no `xor reg,reg` zero
// idiom yet (StdToX64Conversion emits `movRegImm32 0`), so only the two `movRegImm` forms are
// rematerializable here; rdata/witness-table address remat is a later-milestone class.
//
// ALGORITHM (iterative Belady/MIN with the dominating-reload restriction): recompute
// liveness; while some program point OVERFLOWS ITS POOL, find that PEAK op, choose a
// cold-spillable value crossing it — a constant to rematerialize if one is available, else
// the value with the FARTHEST next use (Belady/MIN) — and split its live range AT the peak;
// repeat. Splitting at the peak makes the victim DEAD across the peak op, so pressure there
// drops by one and the loop converges (a store/reload only READS an already-live value / only
// SHIFTS which value is live, so neither ever raises the function's max pressure).
//
// "OVERFLOWS ITS POOL" is per-point, because a FIXED-REGISTER point has a REDUCED pool: at a
// call the caller-saved registers are clobbered, so a value live ACROSS it can only sit in the
// callee-saved subset; at an `idiv` RAX/RDX are the div's own, so a value live across it is
// confined to `pool ∖ {RAX,RDX}`. The reduced pool at an op is `pool ∖ op.implicitDefs` — read
// straight off the operand model, so calls, `idiv`, and `cqo` are handled uniformly with no
// x64-specific mask here. `maxPressure ≤ pool` is therefore NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT; the
// peak-finder (`analyzePressure`) checks each op against its OWN reduced pool.
//
// A NECESSARY-simplicity note: unlike the design's single-sweep Braun–Hack MIN, this recomputes
// liveness once per split. For M5's programs (the splitter fires only on genuinely pressured
// functions and spills a handful of values) that is correct and cheap; a single-sweep rewrite
// is the natural Phase-F performance follow-on (documented, not skipped).

// The fresh value id a `loadRegSlot` defines, paired with the slot it reads and the ORIGINAL
// spilled value it reloads — the AllocChecker's teeth against a wrong-slot reload. Sparse
// (one per reload), looked up by the reload's fresh value id, mirroring `reuseCopyForOp`.
export type ReloadOrigin
	export var reloadValueId as ValueId
	export var slot as SpillSlot
	export var originalValueId as ValueId

	export static function create(reloadValueId ValueId, slot SpillSlot, originalValueId ValueId) returns ReloadOrigin
		return Self{reloadValueId: reloadValueId, slot: slot, originalValueId: originalValueId}
	end 'create'
end 'ReloadOrigin'

export typealias ReloadOriginArray = Array with ReloadOrigin

// The origin record for a reload's fresh value id, or `none` when the value is not a reload
// (a normal SSA value the checker interprets without slot tracking).
export union ReloadOriginLookup
	none
	origin(slot SpillSlot, originalValueId ValueId)
end 'ReloadOriginLookup'

export function reloadOriginOf(origins ReloadOriginArray, reloadValueId ValueId) returns ReloadOriginLookup
	for o in origins 'each'
		if o.reloadValueId == reloadValueId 'match'
			return ReloadOriginLookup.origin(o.slot, originalValueId: o.originalValueId)
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	return ReloadOriginLookup.none
end 'reloadOriginOf'

// The outcome of a lineage lookup: `source(id)` for a splitter-minted fresh value (the
// value it derives from), `none` for any value the splitter did not mint. A union rather
// than a sentinel id — a real ValueId 0 would be indistinguishable from "not found".
export union SplitLineageLookup
	none
	source(sourceId ValueId)
end 'SplitLineageLookup'

// SplitLineage — maps every fresh ValueId the splitter mints back to the ORIGINAL user value
// it derives from: a reload's `loadRegSlot` def -> the value it reloads, a rematerialized
// constant's re-emitted def -> the constant it re-emits. This is what lets the E5001
// diagnostic resolve a fresh id in a blocking set to a SOURCE origin (Rule 3): a fresh id has
// no `ValueOrigin` (it is minted after parsing) and is not a phi, so without this chase it
// would hit the Rule-3 "no origin" panic even though the value the author wrote — the spilled
// variable, the constant — is right there in source. The chase is transitive (a reload of a
// reload, a value spilled then its reload rematerialized), which the diagnostic follows to a
// value that DOES have a source origin (the original constant / spilled user value / a phi).
//
// Two dense scalar columns (fresh id, source id), pushed in lockstep, scanned linearly — the
// COLD path (only the diagnostic asks), and a function mints a handful of fresh ids.
export type SplitLineage
	var freshIds as ValueIdArray
	var sourceIds as ValueIdArray

	export static function create() returns SplitLineage
		return Self{freshIds: ValueIdArray.create(), sourceIds: ValueIdArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Record that `freshId` (a reload or rematerialized def) derives from `sourceId`. The two
	// columns are only ever pushed together here, so they stay the same length by construction.
	export function record(freshId ValueId, sourceId ValueId)
		self.freshIds.push(freshId)
		self.sourceIds.push(sourceId)
	end 'record'

	// The value `freshId` derives from, or `none` when `freshId` is not splitter-minted (a
	// parser value / phi). First match is authoritative — each fresh id is recorded once.
	export function sourceOf(freshId ValueId) returns SplitLineageLookup
		for i in 0 upto self.freshIds.count() 'each'
			let fresh = try self.freshIds.get(i) otherwise panic("SplitLineage.sourceOf: freshIds.get OOB at {i}")
			if fresh == freshId 'match'
				return SplitLineageLookup.source(try self.sourceIds.get(i) otherwise panic("SplitLineage.sourceOf: sourceIds.get OOB at {i}"))
			end 'match'
		end 'each'
		return SplitLineageLookup.none
	end 'sourceOf'
end 'SplitLineage'

// What the splitter produced for one function: the reload origins (for the checker) and the
// number of slots it allocated (each spilled value gets its own — no slot coalescing yet, a
// future optimization). `slotCount * SpillSlotBytes == func.stackSize` by construction.
export type SpillPlan
	export var origins as ReloadOriginArray
	export var slotCount as SpillSlot

	export static function create(origins ReloadOriginArray, slotCount SpillSlot) returns SpillPlan
		return Self{origins: origins, slotCount: slotCount}
	end 'create'

	export static function empty() returns SpillPlan
		return Self{origins: ReloadOriginArray.create(), slotCount: 0}
	end 'empty'
end 'SpillPlan'

// The splitter's result: the final liveness (recomputed on the split IR, `maxPressure ≤
// pool` guaranteed) so the caller reuses it for coloring, plus the spill plan for the checker.
export type SplitOutcome
	export var liveness as LivenessResult
	export var plan as SpillPlan

	export static function create(liveness LivenessResult, plan SpillPlan) returns SplitOutcome
		return Self{liveness: liveness, plan: plan}
	end 'create'
end 'SplitOutcome'

// The peak-pressure program point: the op with the maximum live-before population, its owning
// local block, and the live-before set there (the candidate spill pool). `found` is false when
// no op's live-before count exceeds the pool (nothing left to split).
export type PressurePeak
	export var found as bool
	export var pressure as RegCount
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var liveBefore as BitWordArray
	export var peakSeq as ByteCount

	static function create(found bool, pressure RegCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, liveBefore BitWordArray, peakSeq ByteCount) returns PressurePeak
		return Self{found: found, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: blockLocal, liveBefore: liveBefore, peakSeq: peakSeq}
	end 'create'
end 'PressurePeak'

// The result of the per-iteration pressure sweep: the peak-pressure op (where a victim is
// chosen) and the per-value def-point live count (the pressure each value's spill store would
// face). Both come from ONE backward sweep — a value's live-AFTER-def is captured before the
// backward step, the peak's live-before after it — so the two are never recomputed separately.
type PressureAnalysis
	export var peak as PressurePeak
	export var defLiveAfter as CfgIndexArray

	static function create(peak PressurePeak, defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray) returns PressureAnalysis
		return Self{peak: peak, defLiveAfter: defLiveAfter}
	end 'create'
end 'PressureAnalysis'

// A chosen victim + how to relieve it.
union SpillChoice
	// No cold-spillable value crosses the peak — a HOT overflow (E5001, `raiseRegisterPressure`).
	none
	// Rematerialize a constant (re-emit it before each after-peak use, no slot).
	remat(valueId ValueId)
	// Spill a value to a fresh slot, splitting its live range at the peak (store before the
	// peak, reload before its after-peak uses).
	spill(valueId ValueId)
end 'SpillChoice'

// Split one function's live ranges so the colorer finds every program point within its pool.
// Mutates the IR (inserts spill/reload/remat ops, rewrites reloaded uses to fresh values) and
// returns the final liveness + the spill plan. Called by `allocateFunction` BEFORE coloring.
//
// `throws CompileError` because a HOT overflow — a program point over its pool where NO
// crossing value is cold-spillable — is E5001 (`raiseRegisterPressure`), not a placeholder
// panic. `project` (its `opRanges`/`valueOrigins` tables) + `funcIndex` are what the E5001
// diagnostic maps each blocking Target ValueId back to its source span through.
export function splitLiveRanges(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, pool RegMask, stats RegAllocStats, project Project) returns SplitOutcome throws CompileError
	let poolSize = regMaskPopcount(pool)
	var origins = ReloadOriginArray.create()
	// Fresh-id -> source-value map for the E5001 diagnostic: every reload/remat id the loop
	// below mints is recorded here so a fresh id in a blocking set resolves to source, not a
	// Rule-3 panic (see SplitLineage). Built alongside `origins` (which the AllocChecker needs
	// for the reload SLOT identity `SplitLineage` does not carry).
	var lineage = SplitLineage.create()
	var nextSlot = 0 as SpillSlot
	var liveness = computeLiveness(module, func: func)
	var nextValueId = liveness.valueCount

	// TERMINATION. Each iteration splits one value at the worst OVER-POOL op: the victim goes to
	// memory ACROSS that op (dead there), so the op's pressure drops by exactly one, while NO other
	// op's pressure rises (a store/reload only renames an already-live value over a region it was
	// live in anyway — it never lifts the function's max pressure). So the potential
	// Φ = Σ_ops max(0, effectivePressure(op) − reducedPool(op)) — a non-negative integer — STRICTLY
	// DECREASES every iteration and the loop MUST reach Φ = 0 (no over-pool op → `peak.found` false).
	// This holds under MULTIPLE splits per lineage too: a reload is itself an ordinary value the next
	// iteration may split again (via a fresh id), each such split cutting a strictly smaller run of
	// uses. Φ ≤ Σ_ops effectivePressure(op) ≤ totalOps × (valueCount + 1) bounds the split count, so
	// this cannot be reached by correct convergence — it converts a hypothetical non-terminating
	// splitter bug into a loud panic rather than a hang. (`valueCount` grows as fresh reload ids are
	// minted, but the split count is bounded by the INITIAL Φ, which the initial figures bound.)
	let totalOps = totalTargetOpCount(module, cfg: liveness.cfg)
	let iterationBound = totalOps * (liveness.valueCount + 1) + RegisterFileSize
	var iterations = 0 as ByteCount

	// `analyzePressure` finds the worst OVER-POOL point — an op whose effective pressure exceeds
	// its OWN pool (the full pool for a normal op, the reduced `pool ∖ implicitDefs` at a fixed-
	// register point), with the same reuse-transient + dead-phi corrections `maxPressure` carries
	// so the peak-finder and this driver agree. `peak.found == false` means NO point overflows —
	// the colorer will succeed. It is recomputed each iteration because a prior spill lowers a
	// later def's live-after (an earlier-stored sibling frees a register before this def).
	var analysis = analyzePressure(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool)
	while analysis.peak.found 'relievePressure'
		if iterations >= iterationBound 'runaway'
			panic("splitLiveRanges: '{func.name}' did not converge after {iterations} splits (peak effective pressure {analysis.peak.pressure} still over its pool) — a splitter bug")
		end 'runaway'
		iterations = iterations + 1

		nextValueId = maxOfCount(nextValueId, b: liveness.valueCount)
		let peak = analysis.peak

		let choice = chooseVictim(module, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, defLiveAfter: analysis.defLiveAfter, poolSize: poolSize)
		match choice 'act'
			none then try raiseRegisterPressure(project, module: module, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, pool: pool, lineage: lineage)
			remat(v) then rematerializeConstant(module, liveness: liveness, valueId: v, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, nextValueId: nextValueId, stats: stats, lineage: lineage)
			spill(v) then nextSlot = spillValue(module, liveness: liveness, valueId: v, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, slot: nextSlot, origins: origins, nextValueId: nextValueId, stats: stats, lineage: lineage)
		end 'act'

		// The mutators minted fresh value ids beginning at `nextValueId`; the next
		// `computeLiveness` re-derives the true value count (which now includes them), and the
		// `maxOfCount` re-seed at the loop top advances `nextValueId` past them — so each
		// iteration keeps minting fresh with no manual bookkeeping.
		liveness = computeLiveness(module, func: func)
		analysis = analyzePressure(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool)
	end 'relievePressure'

	func.stackSize = nextSlot * SpillSlotBytes

	// Post-condition (design Phase 4.6): after splitting, the colorer CANNOT fail because NO
	// program point overflows its pool. The loop exits precisely on `not analysis.peak.found`,
	// which is that guarantee for BOTH the full pool (normal ops) and every reduced pool
	// (fixed-register points). `liveness.maxPressure` is an INDEPENDENT cross-check of the
	// full-pool half — it comes from TargetLiveness, a different computation than
	// `analyzePressure` — so a full-pool overflow the peak-finder somehow missed still fails
	// here rather than as a colorer panic. (The reduced-pool half has no separate maxPressure
	// analogue; the loop's `analyzePressure`-based exit is its guarantee.)
	if liveness.maxPressure > poolSize 'residual'
		panic("splitLiveRanges: '{func.name}' finished with maxPressure {liveness.maxPressure} still > pool {poolSize} — the splitter's post-condition (every point within its pool) was violated")
	end 'residual'

	return SplitOutcome.create(liveness, plan: SpillPlan.create(origins, slotCount: nextSlot))
end 'splitLiveRanges'

// A monotone helper for the fresh-id counter (a value count and a value id share the
// `int(0 to u64.max)` domain).
function maxOfCount(a ValueId, b ValueId) returns ValueId
	return a if a >= b else b
end 'maxOfCount'


// ==========================================================================
// The per-iteration pressure sweep (peak + def-point pressures)
// ==========================================================================

// ONE backward sweep computing both quantities a spill decision needs: the peak OVER-POOL op
// (where a victim is chosen) and, per value, the live-set population right after its def (the
// pressure its spill store would face, see `storeFitsPool`).
//
// The peak is the op whose EFFECTIVE pressure most exceeds its OWN pool. Two things make this
// agree with the driver guard rather than false-panicking:
//
//  1. EFFECTIVE pressure carries the SAME corrections TargetLiveness folds into `maxPressure`:
//     the reuse-copy transient (`reuseInputOutlivesOp` — a two-address input that outlives its
//     op costs one extra register for the `mov dest,input` the colorer inserts before it) and
//     the per-block dead-phi count (`deadPhiCountForBlock` — the colorer reserves a register for
//     each dead block-arg across the whole block). Without them a transient/dead-phi overflow
//     enters the driver loop yet finds no RAW peak here — the false panic this replaces.
//
//  2. Each op is compared against its OWN pool — `pool ∖ op.implicitDefs` (`reducedPoolSizeAt`):
//     the FULL pool for a normal op, the callee-saved subset at a call (its caller-saved
//     `implicitDefs` are clobbered), `pool ∖ {RAX,RDX}` at an `idiv`/`cqo`. So a value live
//     across a call that fits the nominal pool but not the reduced one IS found here, rather
//     than passing the splitter and panicking in the colorer.
//
// Both quantities come from the operand model + op metadata, never hardcoded opcodes, so calls,
// `idiv`, and `cqo` need no special-casing. Mirrors TargetLiveness's pressure sweep.
function analyzePressure(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask) returns PressureAnalysis
	let cfg = liveness.cfg
	let wordsPerRow = liveness.wordsPerRow
	let valueCount = liveness.valueCount
	var liveRow = zeroRow(wordsPerRow)
	var buf = OperandList.create()

	var best = PressurePeak.create(false, pressure: 0, blockLocal: 0, liveBefore: zeroRow(wordsPerRow), peakSeq: 0)
	// A value with no op-def (a phi / a value never defined by an op) keeps this sentinel
	// (`valueCount + 1`, above any real count) so it is never mistaken for store-safe.
	var defLiveAfter = filledColumn(valueCount, value: valueCount + 1)
	var blockBaseSeq = 0 as ByteCount

	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let base = i * wordsPerRow
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		bitsetCopyRow(liveRow, dstBase: 0, src: liveness.liveOut, srcBase: base, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)

		// A per-block constant added to every op's effective pressure — the colorer holds a
		// register for each dead block-arg across the WHOLE block (see deadPhiCountForBlock).
		let deadPhis = deadPhiCountForBlock(module, cfg: cfg, liveness: liveness, blockLocal: i)

		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		let opCount = ordered.count()
		// The sweep runs BACKWARD (to fold live-out into live-before). The GLOBAL sequence number
		// must be the FORWARD layout position so it aligns with `opAtSeq` / `computeNextUseSeqs`
		// (both forward): op at reverse-index `ri` sits at forward position `opCount - 1 - ri`.
		for ri in 0 upto opCount 'eachOpRev'
			let forwardPos = opCount - 1 - ri
			let opIdx = try ordered.get(forwardPos) otherwise panic("analyzePressure: ordered.get OOB")
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("analyzePressure: opIdx not in module.ops")
			targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)

			// `liveRow` here is this op's live-AFTER set (the store point for anything it defines).
			recordDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter, buf: buf, afterCount: bitsetPopcountRow(liveRow, base: 0, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow))

			// The reuse-copy transient is decided on the live-AFTER set — BEFORE the backward step
			// re-adds this op's uses — exactly as TargetLiveness.stepBackwardOverOp reads it.
			let reuseTransient = reuseInputOutlivesOp(buf, liveRow: liveRow)

			stepRowBackwardOverOperands(liveRow, base: 0, buf: buf)

			let raw = bitsetPopcountRow(liveRow, base: 0, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)
			let effective = raw + (1 if reuseTransient else 0) + deadPhis
			let reducedPool = reducedPoolSizeAt(op, pool: pool)
			let seq = blockBaseSeq + forwardPos
			// `>=` so that on ties the EARLIER (lower forward seq) op wins — the sweep visits ops
			// in DECREASING forward seq, so the last update at a given effective pressure is the
			// earliest op. Only an op that OVERFLOWS ITS OWN pool is a candidate peak.
			if effective > reducedPool and effective >= best.pressure 'newPeak'
				best = PressurePeak.create(true, pressure: effective, blockLocal: i, liveBefore: copyOfRow(liveRow, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow), peakSeq: seq)
			end 'newPeak'
		end 'eachOpRev'
		blockBaseSeq = blockBaseSeq + opCount
	end 'eachBlock'

	return PressureAnalysis.create(best, defLiveAfter: defLiveAfter)
end 'analyzePressure'

// The size of the pool available at `op` to the values it constrains. At a FIXED-REGISTER point
// the op's `implicitDefs` are unavailable to any value live across it (a call clobbers the
// caller-saved set; `idiv`/`cqo` write RAX/RDX), so the effective pool there is `pool ∖
// implicitDefs`. For a normal op `implicitDefs` is 0, so this is just the full pool size. Read
// from op metadata, so a call, `idiv`, and `cqo` are all handled with no x64-specific mask here.
export function reducedPoolSizeAt(op TargetOp, pool RegMask) returns RegCount
	return regMaskPopcount(pool and not op.rawValue.implicitDefs)
end 'reducedPoolSizeAt'

// Record each of the op's virtual defs as living-after at `afterCount` (its store point). `buf`
// already holds the op's operands.
function recordDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, buf OperandList, afterCount RegCount)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if operandIsDef(o) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'def'
			try defLiveAfter.set(operandValueId(o), value: afterCount) otherwise panic("recordDefLiveAfter: set OOB")
		end 'def'
	end 'eachOperand'
end 'recordDefLiveAfter'

function zeroRow(wordsPerRow ByteCount) returns BitWordArray
	var row = BitWordArray.create()
	row.resize(wordsPerRow)
	return row
end 'zeroRow'

function copyOfRow(src BitWordArray, wordsPerRow ByteCount) returns BitWordArray
	var row = zeroRow(wordsPerRow)
	bitsetCopyRow(row, dstBase: 0, src: src, srcBase: 0, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)
	return row
end 'copyOfRow'

// ==========================================================================
// Choosing a victim (Belady/MIN, with remat preferred)
// ==========================================================================

// Choose the value to relieve at the peak. Candidates are the values CROSSING the peak op
// (live-before it, minus the values the peak op itself uses — those are needed there) that are
// COLD-spillable and NOT already spilled. A rematerializable constant is preferred (re-emitting
// it before each after-peak use is free — no store, no slot); otherwise the value with the
// FARTHEST next use (Belady/MIN), SUBJECT to its store fitting the pool (`defLiveAfter[v] ≤
// pool`): a value whose def-point pressure exceeds the pool has no register-free anchor for its
// store YET — a spilled sibling must free a register first, which a later iteration does (its
// `defLiveAfter` then drops). Remat has no store, so it carries no such constraint. `none` when
// no candidate qualifies → HOT overflow (E5001, `raiseRegisterPressure`).
function chooseVictim(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, poolSize RegCount) returns SpillChoice
	let valueCount = liveness.valueCount
	var usedAtPeak = usesAtPeakOp(module, liveness: liveness, peak: peak)
	let nextUse = computeNextUseSeqs(module, liveness: liveness, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, valueCount: valueCount)

	var bestRematId = valueCount
	var bestRematNextUse = 0 as ByteCount
	var bestSpillId = valueCount
	var bestSpillNextUse = 0 as ByteCount

	for v in 0 upto valueCount 'eachValue'
		if not bitsetTest(peak.liveBefore, base: 0, bit: v) 'notLive'
			continue
		end 'notLive'
		if bitsetTest(usedAtPeak, base: 0, bit: v) 'consumedHere'
			continue
		end 'consumedHere'
		if hasSpillStore(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: v) 'alreadySpilled'
			// v (this exact id) was already stored in an earlier iteration; after that split its only
			// remaining "reads" are before-peak uses + its own store, and it is dead across every
			// later peak — re-picking it would emit a SECOND store of the same id (a double-spill).
			// This is NOT "split at most once per lineage": each split rewrites v's after-peak uses to
			// a FRESH reload id, and that reload is an ordinary value a later iteration MAY split again
			// (it has no store of its OWN yet). So a value crossing K disjoint peaks is relieved by K
			// successive splits down its reload lineage — one store + one reload per peak — with this
			// guard only preventing a given id from being stored twice.
			continue
		end 'alreadySpilled'

		let nu = try nextUse.get(v) otherwise panic("chooseVictim: nextUse.get OOB")
		if isRematerializable(module, liveness: liveness, valueId: v) 'const'
			if bestRematId == valueCount or nu > bestRematNextUse 'improve'
				bestRematId = v
				bestRematNextUse = nu
			end 'improve'
		end 'const' else if isColdSpillable(module, liveness: liveness, valueId: v) and storeFitsPool(defLiveAfter, valueId: v, poolSize: poolSize) 'spillable'
			if bestSpillId == valueCount or nu > bestSpillNextUse 'improve2'
				bestSpillId = v
				bestSpillNextUse = nu
			end 'improve2'
		end 'spillable'
	end 'eachValue'

	if bestRematId != valueCount 'haveRemat'
		return SpillChoice.remat(bestRematId)
	end 'haveRemat'
	if bestSpillId != valueCount 'haveSpill'
		return SpillChoice.spill(bestSpillId)
	end 'haveSpill'
	return SpillChoice.none
end 'chooseVictim'

// True iff `valueId`'s def-point live-set fits the pool. A value idle before the peak anchors
// its store right after its def, so `defLiveAfter[v] ≤ pool` is the store having a register-free
// anchor. (A value used before the peak anchors its store after that use, at pressure ≤ the peak
// and never higher — a store only READS an already-live value, so it never raises the max — so
// this def-point gate is conservative but sound for it: a value cleared here is always spillable,
// and Belady prefers idle values anyway.)
function storeFitsPool(defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, valueId ValueId, poolSize RegCount) returns bool
	let after = try defLiveAfter.get(valueId) otherwise panic("storeFitsPool: defLiveAfter.get OOB for value {valueId}")
	return after <= poolSize
end 'storeFitsPool'

// True iff `valueId` is already spilled — some `storeSlotReg` reads it. The splitter spills a
// value at most once (one store, one reload per after-peak use-block), so this excludes a spilled
// value from being chosen again (a second store would double-spill it).
function hasSpillStore(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("hasSpillStore: op not in module.ops")
			if opStoresValue(op, valueId: valueId) 'match'
				return true
			end 'match'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return false
end 'hasSpillStore'

// True iff `op` is a `storeSlotReg` spilling `valueId`.
function opStoresValue(op TargetOp, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		storeSlotReg(_, srcReg) gives storeReadsValue(srcReg, valueId: valueId)
		prologue to popReg gives false
		loadRegSlot gives false
	end 'k'
end 'opStoresValue'

function storeReadsValue(srcReg X64VReg, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	return match srcReg 's'
		virtual(id) gives id == valueId
		physical gives false
	end 's'
end 'storeReadsValue'

// The set of values the peak op READS (its virtual uses) — excluded from the candidate pool
// because they are needed in a register AT the peak, so spilling them across it is impossible.
function usesAtPeakOp(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak) returns BitWordArray
	let wordsPerRow = liveness.wordsPerRow
	var row = zeroRow(wordsPerRow)
	let opIdx = opAtSeq(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, targetSeq: peak.peakSeq)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("usesAtPeakOp: opIdx not in module.ops")
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'use'
			bitsetAdd(row, base: 0, bit: operandValueId(o))
		end 'use'
	end 'eachOperand'
	return row
end 'usesAtPeakOp'

// The module op index at global sequence `targetSeq` (block-layout order, body then
// terminator). The inverse of the numbering `findPressurePeak` assigns.
export function opAtSeq(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, targetSeq ByteCount) returns OpIndex
	var seq = 0 as ByteCount
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		for pos in 0 upto ordered.count() 'eachOp'
			if seq == targetSeq 'found'
				return try ordered.get(pos) otherwise panic("opAtSeq: ordered.get OOB")
			end 'found'
			seq = seq + 1
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
	panic("opAtSeq: sequence {targetSeq} past the end of the function")
end 'opAtSeq'

// The total number of layout-ordered ops in the function (body ops + terminators across all
// blocks) — the same numbering `analyzePressure`/`computeNextUseSeqs` sweep. Used as the
// "no later use" sentinel: it is strictly greater than every real global sequence number
// (which range 0..totalOps-1), so a value never used again after the peak sorts as the
// FARTHEST next use — the best Belady candidate — while still comparing CORRECTLY under `>`.
// (A `u64.max` sentinel does NOT: on `int(0 to u64.max)` its all-ones bit pattern compares as
// a negative under `>`, so `sentinel > seq` is always false — every "first use after the peak"
// update is skipped and Belady collapses to first-fit, evicting whichever value has the lowest
// id rather than the farthest next use. That silently defeats MIN.)
function totalTargetOpCount(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg) returns ByteCount
	var total = 0 as ByteCount
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		total = total + ordered.count()
	end 'eachBlock'
	return total
end 'totalTargetOpCount'

// For every value, the global sequence number of its FIRST use strictly after `peakSeq`
// (Belady's next-use distance from the peak); the `totalOps` sentinel marks "no later use". A
// single forward pass over the layout-ordered ops (after an O(ops) pre-pass for the sentinel).
function computeNextUseSeqs(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, peakSeq ByteCount, valueCount ValueId) returns CfgIndexArray
	let cfg = liveness.cfg
	let noLaterUse = totalTargetOpCount(module, cfg: cfg)
	var nextUse = filledColumn(valueCount, value: noLaterUse)
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	var seq = 0 as ByteCount

	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		for pos in 0 upto ordered.count() 'eachOp'
			if seq > peakSeq 'later'
				let op = try module.ops.get(try ordered.get(pos) otherwise panic("computeNextUseSeqs: ordered.get OOB")) otherwise panic("computeNextUseSeqs: op not in module.ops")
				targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
				for o in buf 'eachOperand'
					if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'use'
						let v = operandValueId(o)
						let cur = try nextUse.get(v) otherwise panic("computeNextUseSeqs: nextUse.get OOB")
						if cur > seq 'earlier'
							try nextUse.set(v, value: seq) otherwise panic("computeNextUseSeqs: nextUse.set OOB")
						end 'earlier'
					end 'use'
				end 'eachOperand'
			end 'later'
			seq = seq + 1
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return nextUse
end 'computeNextUseSeqs'

// ==========================================================================
// Victim eligibility
// ==========================================================================

// A value is COLD-spillable iff its def AND every use are at loop depth 0, it is not a phi,
// and it is not passed on any branch edge. That exact shape is what Rule 2 needs: the store
// (after a depth-0 def or before-peak use) and every reload (before a depth-0 after-peak use)
// then lie OUTSIDE every loop, so no spill code is ever added to a loop body. A value used
// inside a loop (depth ≥ 1) is deliberately excluded — it is the loop's working set, and if it
// cannot fit that is a HOT overflow (E5001), not something to spill. Phis / edge-passed values
// are the SSA-edge machinery the dominating-reload design leaves to biased coloring, never to
// the splitter.
function isColdSpillable(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	if isPhiValueInFunc(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: valueId) 'phi'
		return false
	end 'phi'
	if isEdgePassed(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: valueId) 'edge'
		return false
	end 'edge'
	return defAndUsesAtDepthZero(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: valueId)
end 'isColdSpillable'

// A value is REMATERIALIZABLE iff its single def is an integer-constant materialization
// (`movRegImm`/`movRegImm32`). Such a value is re-emitted at each use (never spilled), so —
// unlike a spill — it needs no depth-0 restriction on its uses; but it must still not be a phi
// or edge-passed value (those have no op-use site to re-emit before), so those are excluded.
function isRematerializable(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	if isPhiValueInFunc(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: valueId) 'phi'
		return false
	end 'phi'
	if isEdgePassed(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: valueId) 'edge'
		return false
	end 'edge'
	match defOpOfValue(module, cfg: liveness.cfg, valueId: valueId) 'def'
		none then return false
		found(opIdx, _) then return opIsConstMaterialization(module, opIdx: opIdx)
	end 'def'
end 'isRematerializable'

function opIsConstMaterialization(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("opIsConstMaterialization: opIdx not in module.ops")
	return match op 'k'
		movRegImm gives true
		movRegImm32 gives true
		prologue to jcc gives false
		movRegReg to idivReg gives false
		pushReg to loadRegSlot gives false
	end 'k'
end 'opIsConstMaterialization'

// True iff `valueId` is a block-arg (phi) of ANY block in the function.
function isPhiValueInFunc(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
			if arg.valueId == valueId 'match'
				return true
			end 'match'
		end 'eachPhi'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return false
end 'isPhiValueInFunc'

// True iff `valueId` is passed to a phi on any branch edge (a use on an edge, not at an op).
function isEdgePassed(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
			for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
				if argId == valueId 'match'
					return true
				end 'match'
			end 'eachArg'
		end 'eachEdge'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return false
end 'isEdgePassed'

// True iff `valueId`'s def block and every block that USES it are at loop depth 0.
function defAndUsesAtDepthZero(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let depth = try cfg.loopDepth.get(i) otherwise panic("defAndUsesAtDepthZero: loopDepth.get OOB")
		if depth == 0 'depthZero'
			continue
		end 'depthZero'
		// A non-depth-0 block: if it defines or uses the value, the value is not cold-spillable.
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		for opIdx in ordered 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("defAndUsesAtDepthZero: op not in module.ops")
			targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
			for o in buf 'eachOperand'
				if operandIsVirtual(o) and operandValueId(o) == valueId 'touch'
					return false
				end 'touch'
			end 'eachOperand'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return true
end 'defAndUsesAtDepthZero'

// ==========================================================================
// The def / use scans
// ==========================================================================

// Where a value is defined: its owning local block and its op index in `module.ops`, or `none`
// (a value with no op def is a phi or a param-incoming — never a splitter victim).
union DefLocation
	none
	found(opIdx OpIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex)
end 'DefLocation'

function defOpOfValue(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns DefLocation
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		for opIdx in ordered 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("defOpOfValue: op not in module.ops")
			targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
			for o in buf 'eachOperand'
				if operandIsDef(o) and operandIsVirtual(o) and operandValueId(o) == valueId 'def'
					return DefLocation.found(opIdx, blockLocal: i)
				end 'def'
			end 'eachOperand'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return DefLocation.none
end 'defOpOfValue'

// The module op index of the value's def, panicking if absent (callers have already
// established the value has an op def).
function requireDefOp(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns OpIndex
	match defOpOfValue(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId) 'def'
		none then panic("requireDefOp: value {valueId} has no op def")
		found(opIdx, _) then return opIdx
	end 'def'
end 'requireDefOp'

// A single USE site of a value: the op that reads it, its owning local block, and its GLOBAL
// sequence number (forward layout order, matching `analyzePressure`'s `peakSeq` and
// `computeNextUseSeqs`). One entry per use-OP (an op reading the value once or twice counts
// once) — the granularity a split needs to partition uses around the eviction point.
type UseSite
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var opIdx as OpIndex
	export var seq as ByteCount

	static function create(blockLocal CfgIndex, opIdx OpIndex, seq ByteCount) returns UseSite
		return Self{blockLocal: blockLocal, opIdx: opIdx, seq: seq}
	end 'create'
end 'UseSite'

typealias UseSiteArray = Array with UseSite

// Every use site of `valueId`, in layout order, with global sequence numbers. Collected on the
// CURRENT IR BEFORE any spill/remat op is inserted; the `opIdx`s stay valid across insertion
// (`module.ops` is append-only), and each insertion recomputes block positions as it goes.
function collectUseSites(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId) returns UseSiteArray
	var result = UseSiteArray.create()
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	var seq = 0 as ByteCount
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		for pos in 0 upto ordered.count() 'eachOp'
			let opIdx = try ordered.get(pos) otherwise panic("collectUseSites: ordered.get OOB")
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectUseSites: op not in module.ops")
			if opUsesValue(op, valueId: valueId, buf: buf) 'uses'
				result.push(UseSite.create(i, opIdx: opIdx, seq: seq))
			end 'uses'
			seq = seq + 1
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return result
end 'collectUseSites'

function opUsesValue(op TargetOp, valueId ValueId, buf OperandList) returns bool
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) and operandValueId(o) == valueId 'use'
			return true
		end 'use'
	end 'eachOperand'
	return false
end 'opUsesValue'

// The op right after which the spill STORE goes: the LAST use before the peak, or the value's
// def when it is idle before the peak (no before-peak use). `uses` is in layout order, so the
// last entry with `seq < peakSeq` is the last before-peak use. Either anchor keeps `valueId`
// live at the store (it is used there / defined there) and lands the store BEFORE the peak.
function storeAnchorOpIdx(useSites UseSiteArray, peakSeq ByteCount, defOpIdx OpIndex) returns OpIndex
	var anchor = defOpIdx
	for u in useSites 'each'
		if u.seq < peakSeq 'beforePeak'
			anchor = u.opIdx
		end 'beforePeak'
	end 'each'
	return anchor
end 'storeAnchorOpIdx'

// ==========================================================================
// Materializing a spill (Belady split at the eviction point)
// ==========================================================================

// Spill `valueId` by SPLITTING its live range AT the peak (`peakSeq`), so it is DEAD across the
// peak — freeing its register there, which is what lowers peak pressure. The store goes right
// after the value's LAST use BEFORE the peak (or after its def when it is idle before the peak);
// a reload (a fresh value id) goes before the first AFTER-peak use in each use-block, and ONLY
// the after-peak uses in that block are rewritten to it — the before-peak uses keep the original
// value in its original register. The store dominates every reload (it precedes the peak, they
// follow it) and each reload dominates the after-peak uses it serves, so no phi is ever needed
// (the dominating-reload invariant). Returns the next free slot index (`slot + 1`).
function spillValue(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, peakSeq ByteCount, slot SpillSlot, origins ReloadOriginArray, nextValueId ValueId, stats RegAllocStats, lineage SplitLineage) returns SpillSlot
	let cfg = liveness.cfg

	// Snapshot use sites BEFORE inserting anything — the store reads `valueId` too but is NOT a
	// use to rewrite (it legitimately keeps the original value), and the reloads/rewrites below
	// only ever touch the AFTER-peak uses collected here.
	let useSites = collectUseSites(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId)
	let defOpIdx = requireDefOp(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId)

	// STORE: after the last before-peak use (or the def when idle before the peak). It lands
	// before the peak, so `valueId` is dead across it.
	let anchorOpIdx = storeAnchorOpIdx(useSites, peakSeq: peakSeq, defOpIdx: defOpIdx)
	insertStoreAfter(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId, afterOpIdx: anchorOpIdx, slot: slot)

	// RELOAD: one per AFTER-peak use-block. Use sites are in layout order, so a block's after-peak
	// uses are contiguous and follow its before-peak uses; each block boundary among the after-peak
	// uses starts a fresh reload id whose in-block after-peak uses are rewritten to it.
	var freshId = nextValueId
	var reloadCount = 0 as ByteCount
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	var idx = 0 as ByteCount
	while idx < useSites.count() 'eachUse'
		let u = try useSites.get(idx) otherwise panic("spillValue: useSites.get OOB")
		if u.seq < peakSeq 'beforePeak'
			// A before-peak use keeps the ORIGINAL value in its register — never rewritten.
			idx = idx + 1
			continue
		end 'beforePeak'

		// The first after-peak use in this block: one reload before it, then rewrite every
		// after-peak use in this same block to the fresh reload id.
		let blockLocal = u.blockLocal
		insertReloadBefore(module, cfg: cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, beforeOpIdx: u.opIdx, valueId: valueId, freshId: freshId, slot: slot)
		origins.push(ReloadOrigin.create(freshId, slot: slot, originalValueId: valueId))
		// The reload id derives from the value it reloads — record it so a reload id in an
		// E5001 blocking set chases back to that value's source origin (see SplitLineage).
		lineage.record(freshId, sourceId: valueId)

		while idx < useSites.count() and (try useSites.get(idx) otherwise panic("spillValue: useSites.get OOB")).blockLocal == blockLocal 'sameBlock'
			let uu = try useSites.get(idx) otherwise panic("spillValue: useSites.get OOB")
			rewriteOpAt(module, opIdx: uu.opIdx, from: valueId, to: freshId, buf: buf)
			idx = idx + 1
		end 'sameBlock'

		freshId = freshId + 1
		reloadCount = reloadCount + 1
	end 'eachUse'

	stats.valuesSplit = stats.valuesSplit + 1
	stats.reloadsInserted = stats.reloadsInserted + reloadCount
	return slot + 1
end 'spillValue'

// Insert `storeSlotReg(slot, valueId)` right after `afterOpIdx` (a depth-0 def or before-peak
// use — verified cold by `assertPlacementCold`). `valueId` is live at that op, so the store
// reads it from its register.
function insertStoreAfter(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, afterOpIdx OpIndex, slot SpillSlot)
	let blockLocal = blockLocalOfOp(module, cfg: cfg, opIdx: afterOpIdx)
	assertPlacementCold(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: "store")
	var block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: blockLocal)
	let anchorPos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: afterOpIdx)
	let storeOp = TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(valueId))
	let storeIdx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(storeOp)
	try block.opRefs.insert(anchorPos + 1, value: storeIdx) otherwise panic("insertStoreAfter: opRefs.insert OOB")
end 'insertStoreAfter'

// Insert `loadRegSlot(freshId, slot)` right before `beforeOpIdx` (the first after-peak use in
// `blockLocal`). The reload is at a (depth-0) use block — verified cold — and dominates every
// after-peak use in the block (it precedes the first one). The caller rewrites those uses.
function insertReloadBefore(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, blockLocal CfgIndex, beforeOpIdx OpIndex, valueId ValueId, freshId ValueId, slot SpillSlot)
	assertPlacementCold(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: "reload")
	var block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: blockLocal)
	let pos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: beforeOpIdx)
	let reloadOp = TargetOp.loadRegSlot(X64VReg.virtual(freshId), slot: slot)
	let reloadIdx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(reloadOp)
	try block.opRefs.insert(pos, value: reloadIdx) otherwise panic("insertReloadBefore: opRefs.insert OOB")
end 'insertReloadBefore'

// ==========================================================================
// Materializing a remat (re-emit the constant before each after-peak use)
// ==========================================================================

// Rematerialize the constant `valueId` AT the peak (`peakSeq`): re-emit its `movRegImm(32)` (a
// fresh value id) right before EACH use that follows the eviction point, rewriting that use to
// the fresh copy. A constant is trivially rematerializable, so per-use re-emit is fine — it
// holds no register across the gap and never touches memory, so unlike a spill it needs no
// depth-0 restriction on its uses (re-emitting a constant even inside a loop is free). The
// original def is dropped ONLY when no before-peak use still reads it; otherwise it stays live
// from its def to that last before-peak use (dead across the peak either way).
function rematerializeConstant(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, peakSeq ByteCount, nextValueId ValueId, stats RegAllocStats, lineage SplitLineage)
	let cfg = liveness.cfg
	let defOpIdx = requireDefOp(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId)
	let constValue = constValueOfDef(module, opIdx: defOpIdx)
	let useSites = collectUseSites(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId)

	var freshId = nextValueId
	var haveBeforePeakUse = false
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	for u in useSites 'eachUse'
		if u.seq < peakSeq 'beforePeak'
			// A before-peak use keeps the ORIGINAL constant; only after-peak uses are re-emitted.
			haveBeforePeakUse = true
			continue
		end 'beforePeak'

		var block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: u.blockLocal)
		let usePos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: u.opIdx)
		let rematOp = constMaterializationOp(freshId, value: constValue)
		let rematIdx = module.ops.count()
		module.ops.push(rematOp)
		try block.opRefs.insert(usePos, value: rematIdx) otherwise panic("rematerializeConstant: opRefs.insert OOB")
		rewriteOpAt(module, opIdx: u.opIdx, from: valueId, to: freshId, buf: buf)
		// The re-emitted copy derives from the original constant — record it so a
		// rematerialized id in an E5001 blocking set chases back to the constant's source
		// span (the `let`/literal the author wrote), not a Rule-3 panic (see SplitLineage).
		lineage.record(freshId, sourceId: valueId)
		freshId = freshId + 1
	end 'eachUse'

	if not haveBeforePeakUse 'dropDef'
		dropOriginalDef(module, cfg: cfg, defOpIdx: defOpIdx)
	end 'dropDef'

	stats.rematerialized = stats.rematerialized + 1
end 'rematerializeConstant'

// The immediate a `movRegImm`/`movRegImm32` materializes.
function constValueOfDef(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns MachineWord
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("constValueOfDef: opIdx not in module.ops")
	match op 'k'
		movRegImm(_, value) then return value
		movRegImm32(_, value) then return value
		prologue to jcc then panic("constValueOfDef: def is not a constant materialization")
		movRegReg to loadRegSlot then panic("constValueOfDef: def is not a constant materialization")
	end 'k'
end 'constValueOfDef'

// Re-emit a constant with the SAME size choice `appendIntImmToReg` makes, so a rematerialized
// constant is byte-identical to the original (movRegImm32 for u32-fitting values, else movRegImm).
function constMaterializationOp(destId ValueId, value MachineWord) returns TargetOp
	if value <= u32.max 'fitsU32'
		return TargetOp.movRegImm32(X64VReg.virtual(destId), value: value)
	end 'fitsU32'
	return TargetOp.movRegImm(X64VReg.virtual(destId), value: value)
end 'constMaterializationOp'

// Remove the original constant def's op ref from its block (it is now dead — every use was
// rewritten to a rematerialized copy). The op stays in `module.ops` but unreferenced (harmless,
// exactly like the reuse-copy/edge-move ops SsaDestruction appends).
function dropOriginalDef(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, defOpIdx OpIndex)
	let blockLocal = blockLocalOfOp(module, cfg: cfg, opIdx: defOpIdx)
	var block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: blockLocal)
	let pos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: defOpIdx)
	_ = try block.opRefs.remove(pos) otherwise panic("dropOriginalDef: opRefs.remove OOB")
end 'dropOriginalDef'

// ==========================================================================
// Shared op / block plumbing
// ==========================================================================

// Rewrite one op's uses of `from` to `to` (a reloaded / rematerialized copy). The one op that
// reads `from` but must NOT be rewritten is a spill STORE: it deliberately reads the ORIGINAL
// value to write it to memory, and is emitted BEFORE the reload — rewriting it to `to` would
// make it read a value defined later (a use-before-def). Since a `storeSlotReg`'s source is
// ALWAYS an original spilled value (never a reload), skipping every spill store is exactly right
// and touches no genuine use. A no-op when the op does not use `from` at all.
function rewriteOpAt(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex, from ValueId, to ValueId, buf OperandList)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteOpAt: opIdx not in module.ops")
	if opIsSpillStore(op) 'spillStore'
		return
	end 'spillStore'
	if not opUsesValue(op, valueId: from, buf: buf) 'noUse'
		return
	end 'noUse'
	try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: substituteValueInOp(op, from: from, to: to)) otherwise panic("rewriteOpAt: ops.set OOB")
end 'rewriteOpAt'

// True for the splitter's own spill store — the one op reading a value that keeps reading the
// ORIGINAL (not a reload), so `rewriteOpAt` must leave it alone.
function opIsSpillStore(op TargetOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		storeSlotReg gives true
		prologue to popReg gives false
		loadRegSlot gives false
	end 'k'
end 'opIsSpillStore'

// Replace virtual `from` with virtual `to` in a vreg operand; a physical reg or any other
// virtual passes through.
function substituteVReg(vreg X64VReg, from ValueId, to ValueId) returns X64VReg
	return match vreg 'v'
		physical gives vreg
		virtual(id) gives X64VReg.virtual(to) if id == from else vreg
	end 'v'
end 'substituteVReg'

// Rebuild an op with every `virtual(from)` operand replaced by `virtual(to)`. EXHAUSTIVE over
// every TargetOp — the same one-place-per-op discipline as `colorTargetOp`, so a new op is a
// compile error here rather than a silently un-rewritten operand. Ops with no register operand
// (frame / control / call) pass through.
function substituteValueInOp(op TargetOp, from ValueId, to ValueId) returns TargetOp
	match op 'rewrite'
		prologue to jcc then return op
		movRegImm(dest, value) then return TargetOp.movRegImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		movRegImm32(dest, value) then return TargetOp.movRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		movRegReg(destReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.movRegReg(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		movReg32Reg32(destReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		subRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.subRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		imulRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.imulRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		negReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.negReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		cmpRegReg(lhsReg, rhsReg) then return TargetOp.cmpRegReg(substituteVReg(lhsReg, from: from, to: to), rhsReg: substituteVReg(rhsReg, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegRegReg(dest, base, index) then return TargetOp.leaRegRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegRegImm32(dest, base, disp) then return TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), disp: disp)
		imulRegRegImm32(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.imulRegRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		cmpRegImm32(reg, imm) then return TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		xchgRegReg to iatCall then return op
		cqo then return op
		idivReg(divisor) then return TargetOp.idivReg(substituteVReg(divisor, from: from, to: to))
		pushReg to popReg then return op
		storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg) then return TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		loadRegSlot(destReg, slot) then return TargetOp.loadRegSlot(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), slot: slot)
	end 'rewrite'
end 'substituteValueInOp'

// The local block index owning `opIdx` (in its `opRefs` or as its terminator).
function blockLocalOfOp(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, opIdx OpIndex) returns CfgIndex
	for i in 0 upto cfg.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, cfg: cfg, local: i)
		for ref in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			if ref == opIdx 'match'
				return i
			end 'match'
		end 'eachOp'
		if block.hasTerminatorOp() and block.terminatorIndex() == opIdx 'term'
			return i
		end 'term'
	end 'eachBlock'
	panic("blockLocalOfOp: op {opIdx} not found in any block of the function")
end 'blockLocalOfOp'

// The position of `opIdx` within `block.opRefs` (body ops only — spill/reload/remat are always
// body ops, never terminators).
function positionInBlock(block IrBlock, opIdx OpIndex) returns ByteCount
	for pos in 0 upto block.opRefs.count() 'each'
		if (try block.opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("positionInBlock: opRefs.get OOB")) == opIdx 'match'
			return pos
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	panic("positionInBlock: op {opIdx} not in block '{block.label}' opRefs (a terminator or wrong block?)")
end 'positionInBlock'

// ==========================================================================
// Rule 2 verification (the hot-overflow E5001 diagnostic lives in
// RegisterPressureDiagnostic.maxon, thrown from the `chooseVictim` -> `none` arm above)
// ==========================================================================

// Assert an inserted store/reload for `valueId` is COLD per Rule 2: its block is at loop depth
// 0, so it lies in NO loop, and in particular in no loop the value uses. A depth ≥ 1 placement
// would be a HOT spill that should have been reported as E5001 — a splitter bug, not a valid
// spill, so it panics rather than shipping a store/reload inside a loop body.
function assertPlacementCold(cfg FuncCfg, blockLocal CfgIndex, valueId ValueId, what String)
	let depth = try cfg.loopDepth.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("assertPlacementCold: loopDepth.get OOB")
	if depth != 0 'inLoop'
		panic("assertPlacementCold: a {what} for value {valueId} would land in block local {blockLocal} at loop depth {depth} — Rule 2 forbids spill code inside a loop (this is a HOT spill that must be E5001, not a splitter placement)")
	end 'inLoop'
end 'assertPlacementCold'

